diagram: Check Pastinaca sativa traits | Contrast Golden Alexanders | Keep WITHHELD_HOLD
visual: Conceptual botanical diagnostic plate for Pastinaca sativa contrasting Golden Alexanders with HOLD banner.
action: Verify Pastinaca sativa (L.; syn. Yellow roadside parsnip (MN noxious weed)) via Bell Museum Herbarium and USDA PLANTS: Stout grooved hollow stems with alternate pinnately compound coarsely toothed leaves and broad flat-topped YELLOW compound umbels; sap contains furocoumarins. @B01,B12,B14
action: Exclude lookalikes (Golden Alexanders (Zizia aurea — smaller, finer leaflets), Heracleum maximum (cow parsnip — giant white umbels, also phototoxic), Heracleum mantegazzianum (giant hogweed)); reject on: Sap contact followed by sunlight (UV) causes severe painful second-degree chemical burns, blistering, and months-long skin discoloration—even through thin brushed weeds. @B01,B12,B14,B10,B20
action: Separate recognition from eating (SEVERE PHOTODERMATITIS HAZARD — wear long sleeves or pants, wash sap immediately with soap or water, shield skin from sun 48+ hours); obey non-SNA and clean-site rules under WITHHELD_HOLD. @B19,B21,B24
failure: Single-trait guessing or common names confuse Pastinaca sativa with Golden Alexanders, risking poisoning. @B03,B10,B24
verify: Binomial, multi-trait key match, lookalike exclusions, season and habitat, and WITHHELD_HOLD status are logged. @B02,B20,B24
stop: Stop if any key trait is missing or ambiguous; call Minnesota Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) or 911 on suspected ingestion. @B09,B24
reviewer: Medical toxicologist, botanist, and mycologist

Z05-P132 | False Hellebore (Veratrum) & Lily-of-the-Valley (Convallaria majalis) | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C regional atlas)
objective: Document diagnostic traits of Convallaria majalis and Veratrum viride and contrasting lookalikes under permanent consumption HOLD.
applies: Shaded yards, naturalized woods, wet seeps; Spring-Summer (Broad parallel-veined spring leaves (NO onion scent)).
equipment: Flora key, 10x hand lens, metric ruler, notebook.
diagram: Check Convallaria majalis traits | Contrast Allium tricoccum | Keep WITHHELD_HOLD
visual: Conceptual botanical diagnostic plate for Convallaria majalis and Veratrum viride contrasting Allium tricoccum with HOLD banner.
action: Verify Convallaria majalis and Veratrum viride (L. and Aiton; syn. Lethal spring strap-leaf lookalikes) via Bell Museum Herbarium and USDA PLANTS: Lily-of-the-valley: paired smooth parallel-veined lance leaves lacking onion odor, arching stems of fragrant white hanging bells, and red-orange autumn berries. False hellebore: pleated broad ribbed clasping leaves in wet woods. @B01,B09,B10
action: Exclude lookalikes (Allium tricoccum (wild leek or ramps — unmistakable strong onion odor on leaf and bulb)); reject on: Convallaria contains lethal cardiac glycosides (convallatoxin); Veratrum contains lethal steroidal alkaloids causing severe bradycardia, hypotension, and death. @B01,B09,B10,B20
action: Separate recognition from eating (EXTREMELY LETHAL — DO NOT CONSUME; zero onion smell on broad spring leaves triggers immediate STOP); obey non-SNA and clean-site rules under WITHHELD_HOLD. @B19,B21,B24
failure: Single-trait guessing or common names confuse Convallaria majalis and Veratrum viride with Allium tricoccum, risking poisoning. @B03,B10,B24
verify: Binomial, multi-trait key match, lookalike exclusions, season and habitat, and WITHHELD_HOLD status are logged. @B02,B20,B24
stop: Stop if any key trait is missing or ambiguous; call Minnesota Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) or 911 on suspected ingestion. @B09,B24
reviewer: Medical toxicologist, botanist, and mycologist

Z05-P133 | Delayed-Onset Mushroom Toxicity Syndrome Matrix (CDC MMWR Evidence) | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C clinical toxicology)
objective: Recognize why delayed gastrointestinal symptoms (6 to 24 hours) after wild mushroom ingestion signal potentially fatal liver or kidney toxicity.
applies: Evaluating any suspected wild mushroom ingestion documented in CDC Minnesota and 2026 outbreak reports.
equipment: Phone/911, Minnesota Poison Control 1-800-222-1222, clock to record exact ingestion time.
diagram: Log exact time eaten & symptom onset | Flag >= 6-hr delay as red alert | Treat false recovery as emergency
visual: Conceptual clinical timeline contrasting early GI irritants against lethal 6-24 hr amatoxin and 2-14 day orellanine syndromes.
action: Record exact ingestion time and symptom onset immediately: gastrointestinal symptoms starting 6 to 24 hours after eating wild mushrooms are the classic red flag for lethal amatoxin poisoning (Amanita, Galerina) as documented in CDC Minnesota MMWR reports. @B06,B07,B08
action: Never be reassured by a temporary honeymoon improvement on day two after vomiting subsides—in amatoxin poisoning, silent severe liver and kidney failure is actively progressing during that lull. @B06,B07,B08
action: Recognize multi-day delayed renal failure (orellanine Cortinarius, 2–14+ days), neurologic/seizure syndromes (gyromitrin), and rapid muscarinic/GI syndromes; call Poison Control at once for EVERY suspected wild mushroom ingestion without waiting for symptoms. @B06,B08,B09
failure: Waiting at home to see if nausea passes—or going home when vomiting pauses on day two—allows irreversible liver failure. @B06,B07,B09
verify: Exact ingestion time, symptom onset window, and immediate Poison Control/hospital notification are completed without delay. @B06,B08,B09
stop: Stop waiting and call 911 and Minnesota Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) immediately for any suspected wild mushroom exposure or delayed vomiting/diarrhea. @B06,B07,B09
reviewer: Medical toxicologist and qualified mycologist

Z05-P134 | Retaining Uneaten Plant or Mushroom Specimens for Expert Identification | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C clinical workflow)
objective: Preserve remaining uneaten plant or mushroom pieces safely in breathable paper so Poison Control mycologists and botanists can guide hospital treatment.
applies: Immediately following any suspected toxic plant, berry, root, or wild mushroom ingestion.
equipment: Clean dry paper bag or wax paper, pen for time/site label (never sealed plastic).
diagram: Collect uneaten scraps & exact photos | Wrap in dry paper (never plastic) | Bring specimens directly to hospital
visual: Conceptual four-step clinical specimen-preservation workflow ensuring intact samples reach Poison Control and ER clinicians rapidly.
action: Collect any remaining uneaten raw or cooked pieces, trimmings, or intact companion specimens from the exact patch—keeping different species in separate clean dry paper bags or wax paper (never sealed plastic, which rots fungi rapidly into unidentifiable mush). @B08,B09
action: Take clear close-up photos of cap/gills/stem-base or leaves/berries/roots with a ruler if still at the site so a regional Poison Control volunteer mycologist or botanist can review them immediately. @B02,B05,B08
action: Bring the labeled paper-wrapped specimens directly to the emergency department and notify Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) so identification never delays emergency stabilization. @B08,B09
failure: Throwing away kitchen trimmings or sealing damp mushrooms in a zip-top plastic bag destroys crucial diagnostic clues clinicians need. @B08
verify: Intact uneaten pieces sit labeled in dry paper bags and accompany the patient alongside exact ingestion-time notes. @B08,B09
stop: Stop delaying emergency transport to hunt for extra samples; if the patient shows breathing trouble, seizures, collapse, or confusion, call 911 immediately. @B09,M01
reviewer: Medical toxicologist and emergency mycology consultant

Z05-P135 | Poison Exposure Recognition and Emergency Referral (1-800-222-1222 / 911) | New manuscript (Z05 P19-C emergency referral)
objective: Provide clear, fail-closed layperson action steps and immediate escalation for suspected plant, berry, root, or mushroom poisoning.
applies: All suspected toxic ingestions, pediatric mouthing incidents, or severe plant-smoke/dermal exposures.
equipment: Phone, Poison Help 1-800-222-1222, 911, clean water for gentle mouth/skin rinse if awake.
diagram: Call 911 for life-threat red flags | Call 1-800-222-1222 immediately | Never induce vomiting uninstructed
visual: Conceptual three-tier poison-emergency escalation ladder distinguishing immediate 911 activation from Poison Control guidance and ER toxicology.
action: Call 911 immediately if the exposed person has difficulty breathing, airway swelling, seizures, fainting, confusion, blue lips, or collapse—and remove any remaining plant pieces gently from the mouth if awake. @B09,M01,M14
action: Call Minnesota Poison Control / Poison Help (1-800-222-1222, free, confidential, 24/7) immediately for ANY suspected wild mushroom, berry, root, or toxic plant ingestion or child exposure—do NOT wait for symptoms to appear. @B09,B10
action: Never induce vomiting, give activated charcoal, or administer home remedies unless explicitly instructed by Poison Control or EMS; for skin/eye sap exposure, flush gently with cool water and follow Poison Control advice. @B09,B12
failure: Inducing vomiting or waiting overnight after a child eats unknown berries or wild mushrooms worsens aspiration injury and delays life-saving antidotes/supportive care. @B09,B10
verify: 911 or 1-800-222-1222 is contacted immediately, exact ingestion time/amount/specimens are reported, and no uninstructed emetics are given. @B09
stop: Stop all field activity and prioritize immediate emergency medical care whenever toxic plant or fungal exposure is suspected. @B09
reviewer: Clinical medical toxicologist and EMS director
